## <summary>qdistro broker rules.d write surface (companion to qdistro_broker).</summary>
## <desc>
##   The admin broker's SaveRule/DeleteRule RPCs write
##   /etc/qdistro/rules.d — validation tempfile, atomic
##   NamedTemporaryFile+os.replace, rule unlink, and the inotify
##   hot-reload watch. The directory itself is created at install time
##   and read at engine load.
##
##   Why a dedicated type: rules.d historically sat on generic etc_t
##   "so admin can ship rules via config-management" — but the broker's
##   write side then had NO satisfiable grant at all: blanket
##   etc_t:file write would let a compromised broker overwrite
##   /etc/shadow, so the module deliberately never carried it and
##   SaveRule was silently broken under enforcing (no enforcing lane
##   exercised it before the tier-3s clipboard/lineage lanes). This
##   module labels just rules.d as qdistro_broker_rules_t and grants
##   manage on that type only.
##
##   Labelling chain:
##     - The .fc regex types /etc/qdistro/rules.d and everything under
##       it. install-policy.sh restorecon's the dir (it already exists
##       on images where rules were shipped as etc_t).
##     - A name-pinned type_transition covers recreation: any domain
##       mkdir'ing "rules.d" under an etc_t parent gets the right label
##       with no restorecon pass.
##     - Admin-dropped rule files (cp/install into rules.d) inherit the
##       dir's type automatically — they remain broker-readable.
##
##   SaveRule's validation tempdir is created INSIDE the rules dir
##   (dir=target_dir in qdistro_admin_broker.py) so the whole write
##   path needs exactly this one type — no tmp_t grant, nothing the
##   broker could use to scribble on /tmp or elsewhere in /etc. If
##   rules.d is absent, os.makedirs is deliberately DENIED (no etc_t
##   dir-create grant): SaveRule then fails closed with a D-Bus error
##   rather than minting an unlabelled dir the policy cannot see.
##
##   Build note: kernel policy language like tier2/tier3s — compiles
##   with checkmodule/semodule_package, no selinux-policy-devel needed.
##   This module is the SINGLE home of qdistro_broker_rules_t: do not
##   re-declare the type inside selinux/broker/qdistro_broker.te or the
##   two module declarations will conflict at load.
## </desc>
module qdistro_broker_rules 0.1.0;

require {
	# The broker domain (declared by the qdistro_broker module — this
	# module is additive, not a re-declaration).
	type qdistro_broker_t;
	# The /etc/qdistro parent label — source of the name-pinned dir
	# transition below.
	type etc_t;

	attribute domain;
	attribute file_type;

	class dir { add_name create getattr open read remove_name rmdir search watch write };
	class file { append create getattr ioctl open read rename setattr unlink write };
}

# The rules directory itself and its contents.
type qdistro_broker_rules_t;
typeattribute qdistro_broker_rules_t file_type;

# Manage surface for SaveRule/DeleteRule and the engine's inotify watch:
# dir add_name/create/write for mkdtemp + NamedTemporaryFile + os.replace,
# remove_name/rmdir for validation-tempdir cleanup and rule deletion,
# watch for the Gio.FileMonitor hot-reload, open/read/search/getattr for
# listing and engine load. File create/write/append for the rule write,
# read/open/getattr for engine load + ListRules, setattr for the 0644
# chmod, rename for the atomic replace, unlink for DeleteRule and the
# transient tempfile cleanup.
allow qdistro_broker_t qdistro_broker_rules_t:dir { add_name create getattr open read remove_name rmdir search watch write };
allow qdistro_broker_t qdistro_broker_rules_t:file { append create getattr ioctl open read rename setattr unlink write };

# Recreate-proofing: any domain mkdir'ing "rules.d" under an etc_t dir
# lands the dedicated label, so a recreated dir never comes back etc_t
# (which would silently break the broker again). No etc_t file/dir
# manage is granted to the broker itself — see the module header.
type_transition domain etc_t:dir qdistro_broker_rules_t "rules.d";
